From 9ea689059d44074605140220541a84b0f07ea1e4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 00:53:33 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] l2r-local: bug 27: defer a nullable member's release in drop
 glue like a plain rc member

Inside drop glue (`kDropGlueAttr`: the outlined dtors and the
drop_and_free functions), `rewriteDropRc` releases a member box of a
plain shared record through `emitDeferredRelease`: when its count is 1
it goes onto the thread's pending stack, which the outermost drop glue
drains, so a long chain is freed in a loop (patches for bugs 13 and
13b). `rewriteDropNullable` lacked that branch: the non-null arm of a
`Nullable<shared record>` member always emitted a plain `rc.dec`, which
expands into a recursive release. Freeing a chain linked through
`Nullable` therefore took one stack frame (or more) per link: a chain of
`B { a: A }`, `A [value] { b: Nullable<B>, .. }` crashed with SIGSEGV at
1,000,000 links on an 8 MB stack, while the same chain linked through a
plain shared enum frees 3,000,000 links.

In the non-null arm, release the unwrapped box with `emitDeferredRelease`
under the same condition as `rewriteDropRc` (in drop glue, and a plain
shared record box). Outside drop glue nothing changes.

`emitCellRelease` (drop_and_free) releases a freed cell's last non-leaf
record box member after freeing the cell, directly (a tail call when its
count is 1), and defers the others. It picked that member only among
plain boxes, so with the deferral above a record `{ s: S, b:
Nullable<B> }` would release S before B, the opposite of `{ s: S, b: B }`
(and of the order before this change), and a chain through `Nullable`
would cost a push and a pop per link instead of a tail call. A
`Nullable` member whose box is a non-leaf record box can now be that
last member: loaded before the free, then null-checked and released
directly, so both kinds of member are released in the same order.

Generated code changes only where a nullable member is released inside
drop glue: of Reussir's frontend tests, cell_e2e (a Cell<Nullable<..>>
in a shared record) and the new test; the LLVM IR of 30 lean2rr programs
(which have no `Nullable`) is unchanged.

As for plain links, the tail call makes a long chain a loop only when
LLVM eliminates it: at -O none a chain of a million links overflows the
stack through a plain shared enum as well as through `Nullable`, before
and after this change.

Found by the adversarial review of the local patches (RV7P-05; the
release order by RV8R-01); not caused by them (the baseline crashes too).
Test: tests/integration/frontend/drop_long_nullable_chain (two million
links: a shared record linking to itself through a nullable member, the
link inside a [value] member, and the [value] record held in a cell and
released by `cell::set`; -O default and -O aggressive; all three crash
before the fix), and the glue of `{ Os, Nullable<Ob> }` checked to defer
Os and release Ob after the free, like that of `{ Os, Ob }`.
